# New Compiler Data Structures

This is the vision for the compiler after the refactor is complete.
Much of what is details here is based on the work done in the [pup project](https://github.com/alexical-analysis/pup).

## Keywords and concepts

Manta uses a heirachical structure to create the gloabl state that passes through the compiler pipeline.
It contains the following structures for organizing ownership.
- `Context`: This is the global owning structure that all other data structure blong to.
- `Stores`: These actually own the underlying data for the compiler pipeline. They are structured in phases
    so that each major step in the pipeline can access all the underlying data for a given phase with
    a relativly small number of handles.
- `Builders`: Each pipeline phase has a builder that takes the previous phases output and builds it's
    own output. These are named uniqely based on the work they do (e.g., Parser, Noder, Grapher)

The `Context` is the top level type and owns all the underlying data structures through the various stores.
Stores are the actual owning structures and contain `Value` types. Values are the actual data structures
which contain the underlying data. For example in the `AstStore` owns `DeclValue`s, among other types.
`DeclValue` are the actual enum that represents module declarations, use declarations and other top
level Manta decls. For clarity `Value` types always contain the `Value` suffix (i.e. DeclValue).

In order to simplify ownership and lifetimes, data store types do not hand out `Value`s directly. Instead
they return `Key` types which are simple u32 values. These often function as simple indexes into the
underlying store but the internal implementation need not be so direct. Working with indexes makes building
comples relationships and composit structures cheep to copy, and masivly simplifies borrow checking.
Unlike `Value` types which always contain the `Value` suffix for clarity, `Key` types are often just
named after the type they represent. For example, the `AstStore` contains the `DeclValue` type and returns
`Decl` types which are the key types that can be used by the different builders. This is done because
it's more common to work with Key types in complex builder phases.

## Type System

The type system contains 2 broad type categories, these being checked types (`CheckedTy`) and unchecked
types (`UncheckedTy`).
`UncheckedTy` should be used for all types before the checking step has been run.
Part of the job of the type checker is resolve all unchecked types into checked types.
This means that unchecked types represent the results of steps like parsing, and type inference.
These phases may result in unclear, or overly flexible types since only fully type checking can resolve
the concreet underlying types for all expression in Manta.

## Testing

The current test suites in the Manta codebase are far too brittle.
They are so tightly coupled to specific internal details that even small changes to data structures can
trigger huge test changes in dozens of tests.
While the unit tests have been painful, the integration tests have been truely great.
Espeically the pattern where the tests rely on golden files that can be automatically regnerated has
made even larg change to the code base easy to test.
Additionaly, the generated diffs have proven useful in identifying buggy behavior, much more so than
the low level unit tests.
Especially given this is a personal project, I'm going to much more on borad integration tests and leave
the lower level unit tests as future work if they prove useful.
This will initially mean deleting a lot of tests but I think that's fine.

## Compiler Context

The compiler `Context` is the gloabl data structure that owns the results of each phase of the compiler.
Rather than owning all the underlying data directly though, most data is owned by store types.
The `Context` owns the following stores:
- `StrStore`: This is the global string cache where all strings get interred.
- `CheckTyStore`: This is the global store for all checked types in the entire program.
- `UncheckedTyStore`: This is the global store for all unchecked types in the entire program.
- `AstStore`: This stores all the declarations, expressions, and statements produce by the Parser.
- `HirStore`: This stores all the de-surgared code which is a less rich representation, but eaiser for
    downstream passes to work with.
- `GraphStore`: This stores the program as a graph structure with basic blocks and is used for things
    like controll flow analysis.
- `ModuleStore`: This stores all the top level module data for every module in the modules in the program.

It is important to note that the various stores in the context store structures for the whole program,
rather than storing just what is needed for the current module.
This is important for things like type checking where types in one module may be needed to fully infer
data types in other modules.

### StrStore

The StrStore stores each unique string in the program once inside an interal arena.
Because the tokens in a codebase often resue the same strings, doing this reduces the total amount of
text that must be stored during compilation.
It also preserves the ability to compare strings for equality since the resulting `Key` for each interred
string which be the same for identical strings.
Important types for this store are:
- `MStr`: for Manta string, it's the `Key` type which is stable for string comparison.
- `&str`: The `StrStore` is the structure that actually owns the string data so all phases that actually
    need the string bytes can only access them by borrowing.

### UncheckedTyStore

The `UncheckedTyStore` contains all types produced in phases before type checking has taken place.
Like the `StrStore` this uses an internal arena where unique types are ownly stored a single time.
This means the the `UncheckedTy` can be used to quickly check for type equality.
Important types for this store are:
- `UncheckedTy`: This is the `Key` for `UncheckTyValue` and can be used to directly compare types for equality.
- `UncheckedTyValue`: This enum can be use to represent any type in a Manta program including types yet
    to be infered such as the `UncheckTyValue::Any` type.

### CheckedTyStore

The `CheckedTyStore` contains all types that have been succefully infered and type checked by the Manta
type checker.
Only concreet, sizable types can be represented as `CheckedTyValue` structs.
The `CheckTyStore` uses an internal arena to sotore uniqe types only once.
This means the `CheckedTy` can be used to quickly check for type equality.
Important types for this store are:
- `CheckedTy`: This is the `Key` for `CheckedTyValue` and can be used to directly compare types for equality.
- `CheckedTyValue`: This enum can be used to represent any concreet type in a Manta program. The types
    must be sizable and and can not be waiting for inference.

### AstStore

The `AstStore` is used by the parser to build the initial AST representation of a module.
It contains top level declarations, as well as the expressions and statements that build up those declarations.
The `AstStore` also contains positional data for these structures so that diagnositc information can
be correctly provided.
Important types for this store are:
- `Pos`: This is a u32 that points to the canonical starting point for a declaration, statment or expression
    in the whole code base. The same byte offset in different files will have a unique position so `Pos`
    can be used to determine both the starting byte of the fragment as well as the file that contains it.
- `Decl`: The `Key` type for `DeclValue`.
- `DeclValue`: Represents a top level Manta declaration.
- `Stmt`: The `Key` for the `StmtValue`.
- `StmtValue`: Represents a statement in the Manta language.
- `Expr`: The `Key` type for the `ExprValue`.
- `ExprValue`: Represents an expression in the Manta language.

### HirStore

The `HirStore` is a lot like the `AstStore`.
The main difference is that while the `AstStore` tends to map closely to the actual source code the
`HirStore` is a lower level representation of the code.
The `HirStore` contains the de-sugard, standarized representation of the code.
The goal with this pase is to simplify future phases by giving them a less nuanced tree to walk.
Important types for this store are similar to the `AstStore` and include:
- `Decl`: The `Key` type for `DeclValue`.
- `DeclValue`: Represents a top level Manta declaration.
- `Stmt`: The `Key` for the `StmtValue`
- `StmtValue`: Represents a statement in the Manta language.
- `Expr`: The `Key` type for the `ExprValue`.
- `ExprValue`: Represents an expression in the Manta language.

### GraphStore

The `GraphStore` store the program as a control flow graph.
Like other compilers this changes the tree structure of the early Manta phases into a web of connected Nodes.
In this case the Nodes are modeld as basic blocks and all the edges represent controll flow structures.
Examples include function `return`s, `break` statments and the Manta `defer`.
This allows the complier to build the correct controll flow for the block based `defer` which can get
very complex, as well as validating that `match` statments are exaustive and that all functions return.
Important types for this store are:
- `Inst`: The `Key` type for the `InstValue`.
- `InstValue`: An instruction in a Manta basic block.
- `Local`: The `Key` type for the `LocalValue`.
- `LocalValue`: Represents local value in a Manta function.
- `Func`: The `Key` type for the `FuncValue`.
- `FuncValue`: This is a Manta function in the control flow graph.
- `Block`: The `Key` type for a basic block in a Manta function.
- `BlockValue`: This is a basic block in a Manta function. This represents a node in the control flow graph.
- `Terminator`: The `Key` type for the `TerminatorValue`.
- `TerminatorValue`: This is a terminator for a basic block. This represents an edge in the control flow graph.

### ModuleStore

A Manta program is made up of modules which can be imported into other modules creating a dependency DAG.
In order to support single pass compilation, import cycles are not allowed.
The module store tracks all the modules in a program and how they relate to each other.
The module sture itself is just a simple vector of `ModuleValue`s which are keyed by `Module`s.
The imporant structure here is the `ModuleValue` which contains the following data.
- `name`: The `MStr` for the module name.
- `deps`: A map of module alias names (`MStr`s) to `Module`s.
- `path`: The data needed to locate the module in the file heirarchy.
- `ast`: This is a list of the `ast::Decl` that make up the module.
- `hir`: This is a list of the `hir::Decl` that make up the module.
- `func`: This is a list of the `Func`s that make up the module.
